1
00:00:00,120 --> 00:00:01,915
Could you find one number in a million

2
00:00:01,915 --> 00:00:04,378
entries with about twenty comparisons?

3
00:00:04,578 --> 00:00:07,696
Yes, if the entries are sorted. Binary search

4
00:00:07,696 --> 00:00:09,503
checks the middle item first.

5
00:00:09,703 --> 00:00:12,178
If the target is larger, everything below the

6
00:00:12,178 --> 00:00:13,128
middle is out?

7
00:00:13,328 --> 00:00:15,999
Right. Keep the half that could contain the

8
00:00:15,999 --> 00:00:19,780
target, then check its middle. Every comparison cuts

9
00:00:19,780 --> 00:00:22,128
the remaining search space roughly in half.

10
00:00:22,328 --> 00:00:24,554
So doubling the list adds only about one

11
00:00:24,554 --> 00:00:25,712
more comparison?

12
00:00:25,912 --> 00:00:29,360
Exactly. That is logarithmic time, written O of

13
00:00:29,360 --> 00:00:32,384
log n. But remember the catch: ordinary binary

14
00:00:32,384 --> 00:00:35,197
search needs sorted data with efficient access to

15
00:00:35,197 --> 00:00:36,003
its middle.
